// Day 26 — does one operation actually span API, worker and database?
//
// WHAT THIS IS PROVING, and why the pieces existing is not the same as it
// working. QuotesApi publishes to Service Bus and consumes from it in hosted
// services, so the write path crosses an asynchronous boundary:
//
//   HTTP request -> SQL write -> outbox -> Service Bus publish
//        ... later, in the consumer ...
//   Service Bus receive -> handler -> SQL write
//
// Nothing about that is automatic. A trace survives the hop only because
// ServiceBusQuoteEventPublisher writes Activity.Current.Id into
// ApplicationProperties["traceparent"], and QuoteEventProcessorService reads
// it back and calls activity.SetParentId(traceparent). Miss either half and
// you get two unrelated traces that look completely healthy on their own —
// which is the failure this query exists to rule out.
//
// The consumer span arrives as an AppRequest, not a dependency: Azure Monitor
// maps a span of kind Consumer to a request, because from the worker's point
// of view receiving a message IS the incoming operation. So a correctly
// stitched write shows TWO AppRequests under one OperationId — the HTTP call
// and the message handling — which looks like double-counting until you know
// to expect it.
//
// Candidates are picked by looking for operations that touched Service Bus,
// because those are the only ones that crossed the boundary at all. A GET
// never leaves the API and would prove nothing.
// CANDIDATE SELECTION CORRECTED AGAINST WHAT THE APP ACTUALLY EMITS, and this
// is a finding rather than a tidy-up.
//
// This looked for dependencies whose DependencyType mentions "Service Bus" or
// whose Target is the namespace, which is what the Azure SDK's own producer
// span would look like. NO SUCH SPAN EXISTS in this deployment. The
// dependency breakdown over the same window lists SQL, InProc,
// Microsoft.AAD, HTTP to the IMDS token endpoint, and:
//
//   Other | Outbox publish | 1 call | 461ms
//
// That is the app's OWN span, from QuotesActivitySource, wrapping the publish.
// The Azure.Messaging.ServiceBus spans are absent even though
// ObservabilityExtensions calls AddSource("Azure.Messaging.ServiceBus") --
// most likely because the SDK names its sources per client type
// (Azure.Messaging.ServiceBus.ServiceBusSender and friends) and AddSource
// matches exactly rather than by prefix, so a wildcard is needed. Recorded as
// a real gap in instrumentation coverage: the hop is traced by our span, not
// by the SDK's, which is thinner than intended.
//
// AND THE WINDOW WAS TOO NARROW. ago(1h) excluded the write entirely once
// half an hour of debugging had passed -- the "NOT CONFIRMED" verdict was
// partly just a stale clock. 24h matches the other queries in this pack.
let ops =
    AppDependencies
    | where TimeGenerated > ago(24h)
    | where DependencyType has "Service Bus"
         or Target has "servicebus.windows.net"
         or Name has "Outbox publish"
    | distinct OperationId;
union
    (AppRequests
        | where TimeGenerated > ago(24h) and OperationId in (ops)
        | extend Kind = "request", Detail = Name),
    (AppDependencies
        | where TimeGenerated > ago(24h) and OperationId in (ops)
        | extend Kind = "dependency", Detail = strcat(DependencyType, " :: ", Name))
| project TimeGenerated, OperationId, Kind, Detail, Target, DurationMs, SpanId = Id, ParentSpanId = ParentId
| order by OperationId asc, TimeGenerated asc
//
// READING THE RESULT. One OperationId should carry, in order: the HTTP
// request; a SQL dependency; a Service Bus publish dependency; then a second
// request (the consumer) whose ParentSpanId matches the publish span; then
// the worker's own SQL dependency. If the second request has an empty or
// unrelated ParentSpanId, traceparent is not surviving the hop and the two
// halves are separate traces wearing the same clothes.
